{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:a830572b3a1ab2b648c4af8224f10c4f236de592e5d38ae719a5f529ddd50c81","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.49},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"RESOLVING SERIES. Conjunct (a): no official \"OECD-38 GDP-weighted core\" exists; OECD.Stat publishes an OECD-total inflation aggregate that is PPP-GDP-weighted, and the Section-locked accounting basis (constant 2025 USD PPP) forces PPP weights absent other instruction. Conjunct (b): China NBS monthly headline CPI, basket reweighted on years ending 0 and 5 (2026 and 2031 rebases fall inside the window).\n\nTHE DOMINANT ARTEFACT (index construction, worth +10 to +20pp, direction YES). A PPP-GDP-weighted OECD-38 core aggregate carries Türkiye at ~5% of OECD-38 PPP GDP (vs ~1.3% at market rates). Türkiye core CPI (TÜİK ex food/energy) was ~55% in 2024 and ~30% in 2025; even a 2027-36 average of 12-18% contributes +0.6 to +0.9pp to the aggregate single-handedly. Mexico, Colombia, Chile, Costa Rica, Poland, Hungary add +0.10 to +0.20pp. This is why OECD-total inflation printed 6-7% when G7 printed 2-3%. Underlying G7-ish core of ~2.1-2.3% therefore lands at ~2.7-3.1% on the stated measure. Second artefact, opposite sign and >3pp: \"core CPI/PCE\" is an OR. US core PCE runs 25-40bp BELOW US core CPI persistently (Fisher chaining, broader scope, lower shelter weight); the US is the single largest weight, so a PCE reading lowers the aggregate ~0.10-0.15pp. Third: Japan's conventional \"core\" is ex-fresh-food (INCLUDES energy) and has run 2.5-3%; using the Japanese convention rather than core-core lifts the aggregate ~+0.05pp. Marginal (a) = 0.86 — near-mechanical, not a macro call.\n\nConjunct (b). China CPI 2023 +0.2%, 2024 +0.2%, 2025 ~0.0%. Construction biases measured Chinese CPI LOW relative to OECD practice: owner-occupied housing enters as rent-equivalent at a very small effective weight (residence ~16% but mostly utilities and actual rent), while food sits at ~18-20% so the pork cycle supplies the variance — one bad food year at +5% headline adds +0.5pp to a 10-year average, which is the main threat to (b). Reference class: Japan 1995-2005 averaged ~-0.1%, 2000-2010 ~-0.2%, so a sub-1.5% decade is attainable; against that, the 2026 and 2031 rebases and official reluctance to publish sustained deflation both bias prints slightly up. Marginal (b) = 0.62.\n\nCONJUNCTION. Mild NEGATIVE correlation: Chinese deflation exports tradables disinflation to the OECD, cutting against (a) — though (a) is carried by services plus the Türkiye artefact, both of which are orthogonal to Chinese goods prices. P(b|a) = 0.58. Joint = 0.86 x 0.58 = 0.499. Answer 49. Note the ensemble-relevant asymmetry: almost all of my YES mass comes from the OECD leg being an accounting near-certainty, not from a stagflation view.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.217Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"TÜİK publishes Türkiye calendar-2029 core CPI (ex food and energy, annual average) below 8.0%, and OECD.Stat's OECD-total core aggregate narrows to within 0.25pp of the G7 core aggregate.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31 2030 (Jan-2030 print plus OECD aggregate revision)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes ~0.35pp of the composition floor that carries my CQ2 OECD conjunct and my CQ6-C mass. CQ2 falls from 49 to ~41 (conjunct a from 0.86 to ~0.72); CQ6-C falls ~6pp to ~24 with the mass going to B (~53) and D (~10). CQ4 P50 unchanged.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes SES 2026 median gross hourly earnings by NACE section showing section Q's median fa